var articles = {
// ===== 科学科技 (15篇) =====
science: {title:"How Do Batteries Store Energy?",source:"Wikipedia: Electric battery",text:"Batteries are everywhere. They power our phones, laptops, and even electric cars. Inside every battery there are three main parts: a positive electrode called the cathode, a negative electrode called the anode, and a chemical substance called the electrolyte. When you connect a battery to a device, a chemical reaction begins. Electrons flow from the anode to the cathode through the device, creating an electric current. Rechargeable batteries work differently. When you plug them in, the electric current forces the reaction to run in reverse, restoring the chemicals. The lithium-ion battery, invented in the 1980s, made portable electronics possible and still powers most smartphones and electric vehicles today."},
"science-2":{title:"Why Is the Sky Blue?",source:"Wikipedia: Rayleigh scattering",text:"Sunlight may look white, but it is actually made up of all the colors of the rainbow. When sunlight reaches Earth, it hits gases and tiny particles in the atmosphere. Different colors of light have different wavelengths. Blue light has a shorter wavelength than red light, which means it is scattered much more by the atmosphere. This is called Rayleigh scattering, named after the British physicist Lord Rayleigh who explained it in the 19th century. At sunset, sunlight travels through more atmosphere, and most of the blue light has already been scattered away, leaving the redder colors for us to see."},
"science-3":{title:"The Search for Dark Matter",source:"Wikipedia: Dark matter",text:"Everything we can see makes up only about five percent of the universe. The rest is dark matter and dark energy. Scientists know dark matter exists because of its gravitational effects. Galaxies spin so fast that they should fly apart, but something invisible holds them together. Unlike normal matter, dark matter does not absorb, reflect, or emit light, making it extremely difficult to detect. Scientists have built sensitive detectors deep underground to try to catch dark matter particles. Some theories suggest dark matter is made of WIMPs. Other scientists think it might be made of axions. Understanding dark matter is one of the biggest challenges in modern physics."},
"science-4":{title:"How Does Wi-Fi Actually Work?",source:"Wikipedia: Wi-Fi",text:"Wi-Fi uses radio waves to transmit data between your device and a router. These radio waves are similar to those used by mobile phones or television broadcasts, but they operate at specific frequencies. When you send an email or watch a video, your device converts the data into a radio signal and sends it to the router. The router then forwards it to the internet through a physical cable. The whole process happens in milliseconds. Wi-Fi gets its name from a marketing company and does not actually stand for anything. The technology is based on a set of standards called IEEE 802.11. Each new generation of Wi-Fi has become faster and more reliable. The latest version can handle multiple devices streaming high-definition video at the same time."},
"science-5":{title:"What Causes Earthquakes?",source:"Wikipedia: Earthquake",text:"The Earth's surface is made up of massive pieces called tectonic plates. These plates are constantly moving, but very slowly. When two plates push against each other, pressure builds up over many years. Eventually, the rocks break and release all that stored energy in the form of seismic waves. This is what we feel as an earthquake. Most earthquakes happen along plate boundaries. The Pacific Ring of Fire is the most seismically active region on Earth. Scientists use instruments called seismographs to measure earthquakes. The Richter scale, developed in 1935, was once the standard way to measure earthquake strength. Today, scientists more commonly use the moment magnitude scale, which gives more accurate readings for very large earthquakes."},
"science-6":{title:"The Mystery of Black Holes",source:"Wikipedia: Black hole",text:"A black hole is a region of space where gravity is so strong that nothing can escape, not even light. Black holes form when very massive stars collapse at the end of their lives. The boundary around a black hole is called the event horizon. Once something crosses this boundary, it can never return. At the center of the black hole lies the singularity, a point where matter is crushed to infinite density. In 2019, scientists captured the first ever image of a black hole using a network of telescopes around the world. The image showed the shadow of the supermassive black hole at the center of galaxy M87. This was a major milestone in astronomy and confirmed many of Einstein's predictions about gravity."},
"science-7":{title:"How Do Vaccines Protect Us?",source:"Wikipedia: Vaccine",text:"Vaccines are one of the greatest inventions in medical history. They work by training your immune system to recognize and fight harmful viruses or bacteria. A vaccine contains a weakened or inactive part of a germ. When injected into your body, your immune system responds by creating antibodies. These antibodies remain in your body long after the vaccine is gone. If you later encounter the real germ, your immune system remembers it and can fight it off quickly. The first successful vaccine was developed by Edward Jenner in 1796 to prevent smallpox. Since then, vaccines have saved millions of lives. The rapid development of COVID-19 vaccines in 2020 showed how far vaccine technology has come."},
"science-8":{title:"The Internet: A Brief History",source:"Wikipedia: Internet",text:"The internet began in the 1960s as a research project funded by the United States government. It was originally called ARPANET. The goal was to create a communication network that could survive a nuclear attack. In 1989, a British scientist named Tim Berners-Lee invented the World Wide Web. This made the internet accessible to ordinary people. Before the Web, using the internet required typing complicated commands. The first web browser, called Mosaic, was released in 1993. It let users view web pages with text and images. By the year 2000, hundreds of millions of people were online. Today, more than half of the world's population uses the internet. It has changed how we work, learn, communicate, and entertain ourselves."},
"science-9":{title:"What Are Stars Made Of?",source:"Wikipedia: Star",text:"Stars are giant balls of hot gas, mainly hydrogen and helium. In the core of a star, the temperature and pressure are so extreme that hydrogen atoms fuse together to create helium. This process, called nuclear fusion, releases enormous amounts of energy in the form of light and heat. Our sun is a medium-sized star. It has been shining for about 4.6 billion years and has enough fuel to last another 5 billion years. Stars come in many different sizes and colors. Blue stars are the hottest, while red stars are the coolest. When a star runs out of fuel, it can become a white dwarf, a neutron star, or if it is massive enough, a black hole. The elements that make up our bodies, like carbon and oxygen, were created inside ancient stars that exploded long ago."},
"science-10":{title:"How Does GPS Find Your Location?",source:"Wikipedia: GPS",text:"The Global Positioning System, or GPS, is a network of about 30 satellites orbiting the Earth. Each satellite constantly broadcasts its position and the exact time. Your phone receives signals from at least four satellites. By measuring how long each signal took to arrive, your phone can calculate its distance from each satellite. Using a mathematical technique called trilateration, it then figures out your precise location on Earth. GPS was originally developed by the United States military in the 1970s. It became available for civilian use in the 1980s. Today, GPS is built into almost every smartphone. It is used for navigation, fitness tracking, delivery services, and even helping farmers plant crops in precise patterns."},
"science-11":{title:"The Amazing World of DNA",source:"Wikipedia: DNA",text:"DNA is the instruction manual for all living things. The name stands for deoxyribonucleic acid. DNA is shaped like a twisted ladder, a structure known as a double helix. The rungs of the ladder are made of four chemical bases: adenine, thymine, guanine, and cytosine. The order of these bases carries genetic information, much like how letters form words and sentences. Human DNA contains about three billion base pairs. If you stretched out all the DNA in a single human cell, it would be about two meters long. In 1953, James Watson and Francis Crick discovered the structure of DNA. Their work revolutionized biology and medicine. Today, DNA analysis is used to solve crimes, study ancient history, and develop new medical treatments."},
"science-12":{title:"How Do Airplanes Stay in the Air?",source:"Wikipedia: Lift (force)",text:"Four forces act on an airplane in flight: lift, weight, thrust, and drag. Lift is the upward force that keeps the plane in the air. It is created by the shape of the wings. Air moves faster over the curved top of the wing and slower underneath it. This creates lower pressure above the wing and higher pressure below, pushing the wing upward. This principle is called Bernoulli's principle. Thrust is the forward force produced by the engines. Drag is the air resistance that pushes against the plane. Weight is the downward pull of gravity. For an airplane to fly level, lift must equal weight, and thrust must equal drag. Pilots control these forces using the plane's control surfaces to climb, descend, and turn."},
"science-13":{title:"What Is Artificial Intelligence?",source:"Wikipedia: Artificial intelligence",text:"Artificial intelligence, or AI, is the ability of machines to perform tasks that normally require human intelligence. These tasks include learning, reasoning, problem-solving, and understanding language. Modern AI systems learn by analyzing huge amounts of data. They find patterns that humans might miss. This approach is called machine learning. A more advanced form called deep learning uses structures inspired by the human brain. AI is already part of our daily lives. It powers search engines, recommends videos, translates languages, and helps doctors diagnose diseases. However, AI also raises important questions about jobs, privacy, and ethics. As AI continues to develop, society will need to decide how to use it wisely and fairly."},
"science-14":{title:"The Speed of Light",source:"Wikipedia: Speed of light",text:"Light travels at about 300,000 kilometers per second in a vacuum. That is fast enough to circle the Earth seven times in one second. Nothing in the universe can travel faster than light. This speed is so important in physics that it has its own symbol: the letter c. According to Einstein's theory of relativity, as an object approaches the speed of light, time slows down for it. This effect is called time dilation. Light from the sun takes about 8 minutes to reach Earth. Light from the nearest star beyond our sun takes over 4 years. When we look at distant galaxies, we are seeing light that left them billions of years ago. In a sense, telescopes are time machines that let us look into the past."},
"science-15":{title:"How Do 3D Printers Work?",source:"Wikipedia: 3D printing",text:"A 3D printer builds objects layer by layer, similar to how a regular printer puts ink on paper. But instead of ink, 3D printers use materials like plastic, metal, or even chocolate. The process starts with a digital 3D model created on a computer. The printer slices this model into hundreds or thousands of thin horizontal layers. It then prints each layer one on top of another until the object is complete. This technique is called additive manufacturing. It is the opposite of traditional manufacturing, which often cuts away material from a larger block. 3D printing is used to make everything from toys to airplane parts. Doctors have even used 3D printers to create custom prosthetic limbs and artificial organs for transplant patients."},

// ===== 健康 (15篇) =====
health: {title:"The Science of Sleep",source:"Wikipedia: Sleep and health",text:"Sleep is not just a time when your body shuts down. In fact, your brain and body are surprisingly active while you sleep. During the night, your body cycles through different stages, including deep sleep and REM sleep. REM stands for rapid eye movement, and it is when most dreaming occurs. During deep sleep, your body repairs tissues and strengthens the immune system. Your brain processes and stores memories from the day. Most adults need between seven and nine hours of sleep each night. Teenagers need even more. Lack of sleep can lead to serious health problems, including heart disease, diabetes, and depression. To improve your sleep, experts recommend keeping a consistent bedtime, making your bedroom cool and dark, and avoiding screens before bed."},
"health-2":{title:"How Your Gut Affects Your Mood",source:"Harvard Medical School",text:"Inside your digestive system live trillions of bacteria known as the gut microbiome. These tiny organisms do more than help digest food. They also produce chemicals that affect your brain, including serotonin, often called the happiness hormone. In fact, about 90 percent of the body's serotonin is produced in the gut. Scientists have discovered that the gut and brain are in constant communication through the gut-brain axis. People with a healthy and diverse gut microbiome tend to have lower rates of anxiety and depression. Eating a diet rich in fiber, fruits, vegetables, and fermented foods like yogurt can help maintain a healthy gut. This exciting area of research suggests that taking care of your gut might be one of the best things you can do for your mental health."},
"health-3":{title:"Why Exercise Makes You Happier",source:"Mayo Clinic and NIH",text:"It is no secret that exercise is good for your body. But scientists are finding that it is just as important for your mind. When you exercise, your body releases chemicals called endorphins. These natural substances reduce pain and create feelings of happiness. But endorphins are not the only reason exercise improves mood. Physical activity also increases the production of a protein called BDNF, which helps your brain grow new cells and protect existing ones. Studies have shown that regular exercise can be as effective as medication for some people with mild to moderate depression. You do not need to run a marathon to get these benefits. Even a 30-minute walk, three times a week, can make a real difference in your mental well-being."},
"health-4":{title:"The Dangers of Sitting Too Much",source:"WHO and medical research",text:"Many people spend most of their day sitting: at a desk, in a car, or on the couch. Research now shows that prolonged sitting is linked to serious health problems. It increases the risk of heart disease, diabetes, and even certain types of cancer. Sitting for long periods slows down your metabolism and reduces the activity of enzymes that break down fat. Your muscles become less sensitive to insulin, which can lead to high blood sugar. The good news is that even small amounts of movement can help. Getting up and walking around for just two minutes every hour can reduce the negative effects of sitting. Standing desks have become popular as one way to reduce sitting time at work. The key is to move more and sit less throughout the day."},
"health-5":{title:"How the Immune System Fights Disease",source:"Wikipedia: Immune system",text:"Your immune system is your body's defense against harmful invaders like viruses, bacteria, and parasites. It is made up of a complex network of cells, tissues, and organs. White blood cells are the soldiers of the immune system. They patrol your body, looking for anything that does not belong. When they find an invader, they launch a coordinated attack. Some white blood cells produce antibodies that mark invaders for destruction. Others directly attack and devour infected cells. Your immune system also has a memory. After fighting off an infection, it remembers the invader and can respond faster the next time. This is why you usually do not get the same disease twice. Vaccines take advantage of this memory system to protect you without making you sick first."},
"health-6":{title:"What Happens When You Drink Water",source:"Medical research, hydration studies",text:"Water makes up about 60 percent of your body weight. Every cell, tissue, and organ needs water to function properly. When you drink water, it is absorbed through your stomach and intestines into your bloodstream. From there, it travels throughout your body. Water helps regulate your body temperature through sweating. It lubricates your joints and protects your spinal cord. It helps your kidneys filter waste from your blood. Water even helps your brain function better. Studies show that even mild dehydration can impair concentration, memory, and mood. Most people need about 2 liters of water per day, though this varies depending on climate, activity level, and individual needs. The best way to check if you are drinking enough water is to look at the color of your urine. Pale yellow means you are well hydrated."},
"health-7":{title:"The Benefits of Reading for Mental Health",source:"Research studies, neuroscience",text:"Reading is more than just a way to pass time. Research shows that regular reading has significant benefits for mental health and brain function. Reading reduces stress by as much as 68 percent, according to one study. When you get lost in a good book, your heart rate slows and your muscles relax. Reading also builds cognitive skills. It improves vocabulary, concentration, and critical thinking. Studies suggest that people who read regularly throughout their lives are less likely to develop dementia in old age. Reading fiction, in particular, has been shown to increase empathy. When you read about characters' experiences, your brain responds as if you were living through those experiences yourself. This helps you understand other people's feelings better in real life."},
"health-8":{title:"Understanding Nutrition Labels",source:"FDA and WHO guidelines",text:"Every packaged food comes with a nutrition label, but many people do not know how to read it properly. The label shows the amount of calories, fat, protein, carbohydrates, and other nutrients per serving. One important thing to check is the serving size. A package that looks like a single serving might actually contain two or three. Pay attention to added sugars, which are different from the natural sugars found in fruit and milk. Too much added sugar is linked to obesity, diabetes, and heart disease. Look for foods with more fiber and protein, which help you feel full longer. Also check the sodium level. Most people eat far more salt than they need, which can raise blood pressure. Learning to read nutrition labels is a simple skill that can make a big difference in your long-term health."},
"health-9":{title:"How Stress Affects Your Body",source:"American Psychological Association",text:"Stress is your body's natural response to challenges. In short bursts, stress can actually be helpful. It sharpens your focus and gives you energy to deal with immediate threats. But when stress becomes chronic, it can damage your health. Long-term stress raises levels of a hormone called cortisol. High cortisol levels can lead to weight gain, high blood pressure, and a weakened immune system. Stress also affects your brain. It can shrink the hippocampus, the part of the brain responsible for memory. This is why people under chronic stress often have trouble concentrating and remembering things. The good news is that stress management techniques really work. Deep breathing, meditation, regular exercise, and spending time in nature have all been shown to reduce stress levels."},
"health-10":{title:"The Truth About Vitamins and Supplements",source:"NIH and medical research",text:"Vitamin and supplement sales are a multi-billion dollar industry. But do most people actually need them? For most healthy people who eat a balanced diet, the answer is no. Your body is designed to get nutrients from food, not pills. Whole foods contain fiber and many other beneficial compounds that supplements cannot replicate. However, some groups do benefit from specific supplements. Pregnant women need folic acid. People who get little sun may need vitamin D. Vegans often need vitamin B12. The danger comes when people take large doses of supplements, believing that more is better. Some vitamins can be toxic in high amounts. Unlike medicines, supplements are not strictly regulated in many countries. It is always best to talk to a doctor before starting any supplement routine."},
"health-11":{title:"Why Breakfast Is Important",source:"Nutrition research, Harvard School of Public Health",text:"Breakfast is often called the most important meal of the day, and science supports this idea. After sleeping for eight hours, your body needs fuel to start the day. Eating breakfast gives your brain the glucose it needs to function. Studies show that people who eat breakfast have better concentration and memory throughout the morning. They are also less likely to overeat later in the day. However, not all breakfasts are equal. A breakfast high in protein and fiber will keep you full longer than one full of sugar. Eggs, oatmeal, yogurt, and whole grain bread are all good choices. Sugary cereals and pastries may give you a quick energy boost, but it will be followed by a crash. If you are not hungry in the morning, start with something small, like a piece of fruit."},
"health-12":{title:"The Power of Meditation",source:"Scientific studies on mindfulness",text:"Meditation has been practiced for thousands of years, but science has only recently begun to understand its effects on the brain. Brain scans show that regular meditation can actually change the structure of the brain. It increases gray matter in areas associated with learning, memory, and emotional regulation. It also reduces the size of the amygdala, the brain's fear center. Just a few minutes of meditation a day can lower stress, improve focus, and help with anxiety and depression. There are many ways to meditate. Mindfulness meditation involves paying attention to your breath and noticing your thoughts without judging them. Other forms use mantras, visualization, or body scanning. The most important thing is consistency. Even five minutes every day is better than an hour once a week."},
"health-13":{title:"How Your Lungs Work",source:"Wikipedia: Lung",text:"Your lungs are two spongy organs that fill your chest. Their job is to bring oxygen into your body and remove carbon dioxide. When you breathe in, air travels down your windpipe into tubes called bronchi, which branch into smaller and smaller tubes. At the end of these tubes are tiny air sacs called alveoli. You have about 300 million alveoli in each lung. Oxygen from the air passes through the thin walls of the alveoli into your blood. At the same time, carbon dioxide passes from your blood into the alveoli to be breathed out. This entire exchange happens in less than a second. Your lungs work automatically, controlled by your brainstem, but you can also control your breathing voluntarily. The average person takes about 20,000 breaths every single day."},
"health-14":{title:"The Science of Allergies",source:"Wikipedia: Allergy",text:"An allergy occurs when your immune system overreacts to something that is normally harmless. Common allergens include pollen, dust mites, pet dander, and certain foods. When you are allergic to something, your body produces antibodies that trigger the release of chemicals like histamine. This causes the symptoms we associate with allergies: sneezing, itching, watery eyes, and in severe cases, difficulty breathing. Allergies are becoming more common around the world. Scientists are not sure why, but one theory is the hygiene hypothesis. According to this idea, modern environments are so clean that children's immune systems do not get enough practice fighting real threats, so they overreact to harmless things. Treatments for allergies include antihistamines, nasal sprays, and in severe cases, immunotherapy, which gradually trains the immune system to tolerate the allergen."},
"health-15":{title:"Why Do We Dream?",source:"Scientific research, psychology",text:"Dreams have fascinated humans for thousands of years. Despite decades of research, scientists still do not fully understand why we dream. The most widely accepted theory is that dreams help the brain process emotions and memories. During REM sleep, the brain replays and reorganizes information from the day. This may help consolidate important memories and let go of unimportant ones. Another theory suggests that dreams are the brain's way of preparing for future threats. By simulating dangerous or stressful situations, dreams might help us practice our responses. Sigmund Freud believed that dreams revealed our unconscious desires, though most modern scientists reject this view. Whatever the reason, most people spend about two hours dreaming every night, even if they do not remember their dreams in the morning."},

// ===== 生活 (15篇) =====
life: {title:"The Power of a Morning Routine",source:"Original content",text:"How you start your morning often determines how the rest of your day will go. Many successful people swear by their morning routines. They wake up early, exercise, meditate, or read before checking their phones. But why does a morning routine matter so much? First, it gives you a sense of control. When you follow a routine, you are making a choice about how to spend your time. This feeling of control can reduce stress and anxiety. Second, a good routine builds momentum. Accomplishing small tasks early in the day makes it easier to tackle bigger challenges later. You do not need to copy anyone else's routine. The best morning routine is one that fits your own life and makes you feel good. Start with just one small habit and build from there."},
"life-2":{title:"The Art of Deep Reading",source:"Research by Maryanne Wolf, UCLA",text:"In a world of short messages and endless scrolling, the ability to read deeply is becoming rare. Deep reading is not just understanding the words on a page. It is the process of fully engaging with a text, making connections, asking questions, and reflecting on what you read. Research suggests that reading on screens encourages skimming, while reading on paper promotes deeper comprehension. When you read deeply, your brain builds rich networks of associations. This kind of reading builds empathy, critical thinking, and the ability to focus for long periods. To practice deep reading, try setting aside 30 minutes without any distractions. Put your phone in another room. Choose a book or long article that challenges you. Read slowly, and pause to think about what the author is really saying."},
"life-3":{title:"How to Build a New Habit",source:"Research by BJ Fogg, Stanford University",text:"Building a new habit is hard. Most people give up on their New Year's resolutions by February. But behavioral scientists have discovered techniques that actually work. The key is to start extremely small. If you want to start running, begin by putting on your running shoes every day. Do not worry about actually running yet. Once putting on the shoes becomes automatic, you can add the next small step. This approach is called tiny habits. It works because it bypasses the brain's resistance to big changes. Another effective technique is habit stacking. Attach your new habit to something you already do. For example, after you brush your teeth, do one push-up. The existing habit acts as a trigger for the new one. Consistency matters more than intensity. Doing something small every day beats doing something big once a week."},
"life-4":{title:"The Psychology of Procrastination",source:"Psychological research",text:"Procrastination is not about laziness. It is about emotion. When you avoid a task, it is usually because the task makes you feel anxious, bored, or overwhelmed. Your brain seeks immediate relief by doing something more pleasant instead. This feels good in the short term but makes the problem worse. The unfinished task hangs over you, creating more stress. Research shows that the best way to beat procrastination is to reduce the emotional barrier. Break the task into the smallest possible first step. Instead of write my essay, make your goal open a document and type one sentence. Once you start, the anxiety usually fades. Another effective technique is the Pomodoro method. Set a timer for 25 minutes and work without interruption. After the timer rings, take a five-minute break. Repeat this cycle four times, then take a longer break."},
"life-5":{title:"The Benefits of Keeping a Journal",source:"Psychological research",text:"Writing in a journal is one of the simplest and most effective habits you can adopt. Studies show that regular journaling reduces stress, improves mood, and even strengthens the immune system. When you write about your thoughts and feelings, you gain distance from them. What felt overwhelming in your head often seems more manageable on paper. Journaling also helps you spot patterns in your life. You might notice that you always feel anxious on Sundays or that certain activities reliably make you happy. There is no right way to keep a journal. Some people write pages every morning. Others jot down three things they are grateful for before bed. The most important thing is honesty. Your journal is for you alone, so write what you really think and feel, not what you think you should feel."},
"life-6":{title:"Why Sleep Matters for Students",source:"Sleep research, CDC",text:"Students often sacrifice sleep to study more. But this is actually counterproductive. During sleep, your brain consolidates what you learned during the day. Information moves from short-term to long-term memory. If you do not sleep enough, much of what you studied will simply be forgotten. One experiment found that students who slept after learning a new skill performed 20 percent better than those who stayed awake. Sleep also affects your ability to focus and solve problems. A well-rested student can learn more in two hours than a tired student can in four. The night before an exam, it is better to sleep well than to stay up late cramming. If you must choose between an extra hour of studying and an extra hour of sleep, take the sleep."},
"life-7":{title:"How to Give a Great Presentation",source:"Communication research",text:"Public speaking is one of the most common fears, but it is a skill anyone can learn. The secret to a great presentation is preparation and practice. Start by knowing your audience. What do they already know? What do they care about? Structure your talk around three main points. More than three, and people start to forget. Less than three, and your talk may feel thin. Use stories and examples to make your points stick. People remember stories long after they forget statistics. Practice your talk out loud, preferably in front of someone else. Record yourself and watch it back. You will notice habits you were not aware of. When it is time to present, remember that the audience wants you to succeed. They are on your side. Take a deep breath, speak slowly, and make eye contact with a few friendly faces in the room."},
"life-8":{title:"The Art of Asking Good Questions",source:"Educational psychology",text:"Asking good questions is one of the most underrated skills. Good questions show that you are listening and thinking. They deepen your understanding and make conversations more interesting. There are different types of questions. Closed questions can be answered with yes or no. They are useful for confirming facts but do not encourage discussion. Open questions begin with what, how, or why. They invite the other person to share their thoughts and experiences. The best questions are simple and genuine. Do not try to sound clever. Ask about what genuinely interests you. Follow-up questions are especially powerful. They show that you were paying attention and want to know more. In a world where most people are waiting for their turn to speak, being a good questioner makes you stand out."},
"life-9":{title:"Minimalism: Less Is More",source:"Lifestyle and psychology research",text:"Minimalism is the practice of owning fewer things and focusing on what truly matters. It began as an art movement in the 1960s but has since become a lifestyle philosophy. Minimalists believe that clutter in your physical space creates clutter in your mind. When you have fewer possessions, you spend less time cleaning, organizing, and worrying about your stuff. This frees up time and energy for relationships, hobbies, and personal growth. Minimalism does not mean living in an empty white room. It means being intentional about what you own. Before buying something new, ask yourself whether it will truly add value to your life. The goal is not to own as little as possible, but to own only things that serve a purpose or bring you joy. Many people report feeling lighter and more free after decluttering their homes and simplifying their schedules."},
"life-10":{title:"How to Manage Your Time Better",source:"Productivity research",text:"Time management is not about squeezing more tasks into your day. It is about making sure the most important things get done. The first step is to figure out what actually matters. Make a list of everything you need to do, then identify the two or three tasks that will make the biggest difference. Do those first, before checking email or social media. This technique is sometimes called eating the frog. Tackle your most difficult or important task when your energy is highest. Another useful technique is time blocking. Instead of working from a to-do list, schedule specific times for specific tasks. Treat these time blocks like appointments you cannot miss. Finally, learn to say no. Every time you say yes to something unimportant, you are saying no to something that matters more. Your time is your most valuable resource. Spend it wisely."},
"life-11":{title:"The Importance of Hobbies",source:"Psychology research on well-being",text:"Hobbies are not just a way to pass time. They play a crucial role in mental health and personal development. When you engage in a hobby, you enter a state psychologists call flow. In flow, you are fully absorbed in what you are doing. Time seems to disappear. Flow experiences reduce stress and increase happiness. Hobbies also give you a sense of mastery and progress. Unlike work or school, where feedback can be delayed and unclear, hobbies offer immediate satisfaction. You can see yourself improving week by week. This feeling of competence carries over into other areas of your life. Hobbies can also connect you with like-minded people. Whether it is a book club, a sports team, or an online community, shared interests create meaningful relationships."},
"life-12":{title:"How to Write a Good Email",source:"Professional communication guides",text:"Email is still the backbone of professional communication. Writing clear and effective emails is a skill that will serve you throughout your career. The most important rule is to respect the reader's time. Keep your emails short. State your purpose in the first sentence. Use specific subject lines. A subject like Meeting Thursday is vague. A subject like Requesting agenda items for Thursday's budget meeting is clear and actionable. Think about what you want the recipient to do after reading your email. Make that action obvious. If you need a reply by Friday, say so. Proofread before you send. Typos and grammar mistakes make you look careless. Finally, know when email is not the right medium. Complex or sensitive conversations are better had in person or over the phone."},
"life-13":{title:"Learning a New Language: Where to Start",source:"Language acquisition research",text:"Learning a new language is one of the most rewarding challenges you can take on. It opens doors to new cultures, improves your cognitive abilities, and can even boost your career. Many people start with grammar rules and vocabulary lists, but research shows this is not the most effective approach. The key is comprehensible input. This means reading and listening to material that is just slightly above your current level. You should be able to understand most of it, with a few new words and structures to figure out from context. Speaking and writing are important, but they work best after you have built a foundation through input. Consistency matters more than intensity. Thirty minutes every day is better than three hours once a week. Find content you genuinely enjoy in your target language. When learning feels like entertainment, you are much more likely to stick with it."},
"life-14":{title:"How to Handle Criticism Well",source:"Psychology research on feedback",text:"Receiving criticism is never pleasant, but learning to handle it well is an essential life skill. The first step is to pause before reacting. Your instinct may be to defend yourself, but this prevents you from actually hearing what the other person is saying. Take a deep breath and listen. Try to separate the message from the delivery. Sometimes criticism is poorly expressed, but still contains valuable feedback. Ask yourself whether there is any truth in what was said. If there is, you have an opportunity to improve. If the criticism seems unfair, ask clarifying questions before responding. Remember that criticism usually says as much about the critic as it does about you. Some people criticize others to feel better about themselves. Learn to distinguish between constructive feedback, which is meant to help you, and destructive criticism, which is meant to hurt."},
"life-15":{title:"The Value of Travel",source:"Cultural psychology research",text:"Travel does more than provide a break from routine. It changes the way you see the world and yourself. When you travel, you encounter different ways of living, eating, and thinking. What seemed normal at home suddenly seems like just one option among many. This builds cognitive flexibility. People who have lived abroad tend to be more creative and better at solving complex problems. Travel also builds empathy. When you see how people live in other places, it becomes harder to hold stereotypes and prejudices. You do not need to go far or spend a lot of money to get these benefits. Visiting a different neighborhood in your own city can broaden your perspective. The key is to approach travel with curiosity and openness. Talk to people. Try unfamiliar foods. Say yes to experiences that push you slightly out of your comfort zone."},

// ===== 文化 (15篇) =====
culture: {title:"The History of the Handshake",source:"Wikipedia: Handshake",text:"The handshake is one of the most common greetings around the world. But where did this custom come from? Historians believe the handshake dates back to ancient Greece. It was a way to show that you were not carrying a weapon. By extending an open right hand, two people could prove they came in peace. In ancient Rome, people would grasp each other's forearms instead of hands to check for hidden daggers. Over time, the handshake evolved into a symbol of trust and agreement. Today it is used in business deals, job interviews, and casual greetings. However, customs vary across cultures. In Japan, bowing is more common. In Thailand, people greet with a slight bow and pressed palms. The pandemic also changed how people think about handshakes, with many now preferring fist bumps or elbow touches."},
"culture-2":{title:"The Origins of Tea Culture",source:"Wikipedia: History of tea",text:"Tea is the second most consumed drink in the world after water. According to legend, tea was discovered by the Chinese Emperor Shen Nung in 2737 BCE when leaves from a nearby bush blew into his pot of boiling water. From China, tea spread to Japan, where it became central to the Japanese tea ceremony. This practice emphasizes harmony, respect, purity, and tranquility. Tea reached Europe in the 16th century through Portuguese traders. It became especially popular in Britain, where afternoon tea became a national tradition in the 1840s. The British love for tea had world-changing consequences. In the 19th century, Britain began growing tea in India and Sri Lanka on a massive scale. Today, different cultures have their own unique tea traditions: mint tea in Morocco, chai in India, bubble tea in Taiwan, and sweet iced tea in the southern United States."},
"culture-3":{title:"Why Do We Have Birthdays?",source:"Cultural history",text:"Birthday celebrations date back thousands of years. The ancient Egyptians were among the first to celebrate birthdays, but only for their pharaohs. They believed that when a pharaoh was crowned, he became a god, and his coronation day was his birth as a god. The ancient Greeks adopted the idea and added birthday candles. They baked round cakes to honor Artemis, the goddess of the moon. The candles represented the moon's glow. The tradition of birthday parties for ordinary people did not become common until the 19th century. The song Happy Birthday to You was written in 1893 by two American sisters. It was originally called Good Morning to All and was meant to be sung in classrooms. Today, it is the most recognized song in the English language. Different cultures celebrate birthdays in their own ways. In Mexico, children hit a piñata filled with candy. In Korea, people eat seaweed soup on their birthdays. In many African countries, birthday celebrations are a relatively new tradition."},
"culture-4":{title:"The Story of Chocolate",source:"Wikipedia: History of chocolate",text:"Chocolate has a history stretching back 4,000 years. The ancient Olmecs of Mexico were the first to turn cacao beans into a drink. The Mayans and Aztecs later adopted cacao as a sacred food. They used cacao beans as currency and drank chocolate during religious ceremonies. When Spanish explorers brought cacao back to Europe in the 16th century, they added sugar and honey to sweeten the bitter drink. Chocolate quickly became fashionable among European royalty. The first solid chocolate bar was created in 1847 by a British company. Milk chocolate was invented in Switzerland in 1875 by Daniel Peter and Henri Nestlé. Today, the average person in Switzerland eats about 9 kilograms of chocolate per year. The global chocolate industry is worth over 100 billion dollars. However, many cacao farmers still live in poverty, and the industry faces ongoing challenges with sustainability and fair labor practices."},
"culture-5":{title:"The Olympic Games: Ancient and Modern",source:"Wikipedia: Olympic Games",text:"The Olympic Games began in ancient Greece nearly 3,000 years ago. The first recorded Games took place in 776 BCE in the city of Olympia. Athletes competed in running, wrestling, chariot racing, and other sports. The ancient Olympics were also a religious festival honoring Zeus. Women were not allowed to compete or even watch. The Games continued for over a thousand years until they were banned by the Roman Emperor Theodosius in 393 CE. The modern Olympics were revived in 1896 by a French educator named Pierre de Coubertin. He believed that international sports competition could promote peace. The first modern Games were held in Athens, Greece, with athletes from 14 countries. Today, the Olympics are the world's largest sporting event. The Olympic rings represent the five continents, and the Olympic flame symbolizes the connection between the ancient and modern Games."},
"culture-6":{title:"The Power of Music Across Cultures",source:"Anthropology and musicology",text:"Music exists in every human culture ever studied. Archaeologists have found bone flutes dating back over 40,000 years, suggesting that music is as old as human civilization itself. Music serves many functions across cultures. It brings communities together at celebrations and rituals. It helps parents bond with their babies through lullabies. It motivates workers and soldiers. It expresses emotions that words alone cannot capture. Research shows that music affects the brain in powerful ways. Listening to music releases dopamine, the same chemical associated with food and love. Playing music together synchronizes the brainwaves of performers. Every culture has developed its own musical scales, instruments, and traditions. Yet certain patterns appear everywhere. Most cultures use octaves and fifths. Music in a major key is generally perceived as happy, while minor keys sound sad. These universals suggest that music is deeply rooted in human biology."},
"culture-7":{title:"Why We Tell Stories",source:"Anthropology and literature",text:"Storytelling is one of the oldest human activities. Long before writing, people gathered around fires to share tales of gods, heroes, and ancestors. Stories served many purposes in ancient societies. They passed down knowledge and values from one generation to the next. They explained natural phenomena that people did not yet understand. They built social bonds within communities. The oldest known story is the Epic of Gilgamesh, written in Mesopotamia over 4,000 years ago. Yet the structure of stories has barely changed. Most stories follow a pattern called the hero's journey, identified by scholar Joseph Campbell. A hero leaves home, faces challenges, gains wisdom, and returns transformed. This pattern appears in myths, novels, and movies across all cultures. Stories continue to shape our lives today. The news we read, the shows we watch, and even our memories are structured as narratives."},
"culture-8":{title:"The History of Money",source:"Wikipedia: History of money",text:"Before money existed, people traded goods directly through barter. A farmer might trade a sack of grain for a pair of shoes. But barter is inefficient. You need to find someone who wants what you have and has what you want. The first forms of money appeared about 5,000 years ago. The ancient Mesopotamians used silver bars as a medium of exchange. The Chinese were the first to use paper money, around the 7th century. Before that, Chinese money took the form of shells, tools, and metal coins. The word salary comes from the Latin word for salt, because Roman soldiers were sometimes paid in salt. Today, most money exists only as numbers in computer systems. Cryptocurrencies like Bitcoin have introduced yet another form of money that exists only digitally and is not controlled by any government."},
"culture-9":{title:"The Art of Japanese Bonsai",source:"Wikipedia: Bonsai",text:"Bonsai is the Japanese art of growing miniature trees in containers. The word bonsai means planted in a container in Japanese. The practice actually originated in China over a thousand years ago, where it was called penjing. It was later adopted and refined by the Japanese. Bonsai trees are not genetically dwarfed. They are regular trees kept small through careful pruning of roots and branches, wiring of trunks, and restriction to small pots. A well-cared-for bonsai can live for hundreds of years. Some famous bonsai trees in Japan are over 500 years old and have been passed down through generations of the same family. Bonsai is more than gardening. It is considered a form of meditation and artistic expression. The goal is to create a tree that looks like a mature tree in nature, but in miniature form. Each tree tells a story through its shape and style."},
"culture-10":{title:"How Coffee Changed the World",source:"Wikipedia: History of coffee",text:"Coffee was first discovered in Ethiopia, where legend says a goat herder noticed his goats became energetic after eating coffee berries. From Ethiopia, coffee spread to the Arabian Peninsula. By the 15th century, coffee houses had become important social institutions in the Middle East. People gathered to drink coffee, play chess, discuss politics, and listen to music. When coffee reached Europe in the 17th century, it had a similar effect. Coffee houses became centers of intellectual exchange. They were sometimes called penny universities because for the price of a cup of coffee, you could listen to the smartest people in town debate ideas. Today, coffee is one of the world's most valuable commodities, traded globally and supporting the livelihoods of over 100 million people. The coffee industry faces challenges including climate change, which threatens coffee-growing regions, and fair trade concerns."},
"culture-11":{title:"The Golden Rule Around the World",source:"Comparative religion and philosophy",text:"The Golden Rule is the principle of treating others as you would like to be treated. It appears in some form in nearly every major religion and ethical tradition. In Christianity, Jesus taught: Do unto others as you would have them do unto you. In Confucianism, the rule is stated negatively: Do not do to others what you do not want done to yourself. Buddhism teaches: Hurt not others in ways that you yourself would find hurtful. Hinduism says: This is the sum of duty: do not do to others what would cause pain if done to you. Islam teaches: None of you has faith until he loves for his brother what he loves for himself. The fact that the same idea emerged independently in so many different cultures suggests it is a universal human value. Philosophers call this moral reciprocity. It requires us to imagine ourselves in another person's position, which is the foundation of empathy and ethical behavior."},
"culture-12":{title:"Body Language Across Cultures",source:"Cross-cultural communication studies",text:"The same gesture can mean very different things in different cultures. A thumbs up is a sign of approval in most Western countries. But in parts of the Middle East and West Africa, it is an offensive gesture. The OK sign made by touching the thumb and index finger means everything is fine in the United States. In Brazil, however, it is considered very rude. Eye contact also varies by culture. In Western cultures, direct eye contact signals confidence and honesty. In many East Asian cultures, prolonged eye contact can be seen as aggressive or disrespectful, especially when speaking to someone older or of higher status. The way people use personal space differs too. In Latin American and Middle Eastern cultures, people tend to stand closer when talking than people from Northern Europe or North America. Understanding these differences is increasingly important in our globalized world. What is polite in one culture can be insulting in another."},
"culture-13":{title:"The Evolution of Fashion",source:"Wikipedia: History of clothing",text:"Clothing began as a practical need for warmth and protection. The earliest clothes were made from animal skins and plant fibers. But clothing quickly became about more than survival. In ancient Egypt, the quality of your linen indicated your social status. In ancient Rome, only senators could wear purple. Fashion as we know it today began in Europe in the 14th century. Before then, clothing styles changed very slowly over generations. The rise of fashion was linked to the rise of trade and wealth. As the middle class grew, more people could afford to care about how they dressed. The invention of the sewing machine in the 19th century made clothing cheaper and more accessible. In the 20th century, fashion became a global industry. Today, fast fashion brands produce new styles every few weeks. This has raised concerns about environmental sustainability and labor conditions in the clothing industry."},
"culture-14":{title:"Why We Celebrate New Year",source:"Wikipedia: New Year",text:"The celebration of a new year is one of the oldest and most universal human traditions. The ancient Babylonians were among the first to celebrate the new year about 4,000 years ago. Their celebration lasted 11 days and involved religious rituals and the crowning of a new king. The Romans originally celebrated the new year in March, but Julius Caesar moved it to January 1st in 46 BCE. The month January is named after Janus, the Roman god of beginnings, who had two faces so he could look back at the past and forward to the future. Not all cultures celebrate the new year on January 1st. The Chinese New Year usually falls in late January or early February. The Islamic New Year moves through the seasons because the Islamic calendar is based on the moon. Rosh Hashanah, the Jewish New Year, occurs in September or October. Despite the different dates, most New Year traditions share common themes: reflecting on the past, making resolutions for the future, and gathering with loved ones."},
"culture-15":{title:"The Rise of Social Media",source:"Wikipedia: Social media",text:"Social media has transformed how we communicate, share information, and form relationships. The first recognizable social media site was Six Degrees, launched in 1997. It let users create profiles and connect with friends. MySpace became the largest social network in the early 2000s, followed by Facebook, which was founded in 2004 and now has billions of users. Social media has brought many benefits. It helps people maintain relationships across long distances. It has given a voice to marginalized groups. It has enabled social movements to organize quickly. But it has also created problems. The algorithms that determine what we see are designed to maximize engagement, which often means promoting emotionally charged or controversial content. This can create echo chambers where people only see opinions they already agree with. There are also growing concerns about privacy, mental health, and the spread of misinformation."},

// ===== 自然 (15篇) =====
nature: {title:"The Secret Life of Bees",source:"Wikipedia: Honey bee",text:"Bees are among the most important creatures on our planet. They pollinate about one third of the food we eat. Without bees, our supermarkets would look very empty. A single hive can contain up to 60,000 bees. The queen bee is the mother of almost all of them. Worker bees are female and do most of the jobs: cleaning, feeding larvae, collecting nectar, and guarding the hive. Male bees, called drones, have only one purpose: to mate with a new queen. Bees have a fascinating way of communicating. When a worker bee finds a good source of nectar, she returns to the hive and performs a waggle dance. The angle of the dance indicates the direction relative to the sun. The duration of the waggle tells other bees how far away the flowers are."},
"nature-2":{title:"Coral Reefs: Rainforests of the Sea",source:"NOAA and Wikipedia: Coral reef",text:"Coral reefs cover less than one percent of the ocean floor, yet they support about 25 percent of all marine life. Corals are actually tiny animals called polyps. They build hard skeletons around themselves, which over thousands of years form massive reef structures. Inside the polyps live microscopic algae that produce food through photosynthesis. In return, the coral provides the algae with shelter. When ocean temperatures rise too high, corals expel the algae and turn white. This is called coral bleaching. Scientists estimate that we have already lost half of the world's coral reefs in the last 30 years. Pollution, overfishing, and ocean acidification also threaten these delicate ecosystems. Millions of people depend on reefs for food, coastal protection, and income from tourism."},
"nature-3":{title:"The Water Cycle",source:"Wikipedia: Water cycle",text:"Water on Earth is constantly moving through a process called the water cycle. It involves evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and collection. The sun heats water in oceans, lakes, and rivers, turning it into water vapor. This vapor rises into the atmosphere. As it cools, it condenses into tiny droplets that form clouds. When the droplets become heavy enough, they fall as rain, snow, or hail. Some of this water soaks into the ground and becomes groundwater. The rest flows into rivers and streams that carry it back to the oceans. The water you drink today might have once been part of a dinosaur, fallen as snow on a mountain, or flowed through an ancient river. The Earth has a fixed amount of water. It has been recycling the same water for billions of years."},
"nature-4":{title:"The Amazing Journey of Monarch Butterflies",source:"Wikipedia: Monarch butterfly",text:"Every year, millions of monarch butterflies travel up to 4,000 kilometers from Canada and the United States to Mexico. This is one of the longest insect migrations on Earth. What makes this journey even more remarkable is that no single butterfly completes the round trip. The butterflies that arrive in Mexico are the great-grandchildren of the ones that left the previous spring. Yet they find their way to the same forests where their ancestors spent the winter. Scientists still do not fully understand how monarchs navigate. They appear to use a combination of the sun's position and the Earth's magnetic field. Along the way, monarchs depend on milkweed plants to lay their eggs. The loss of milkweed due to farming and development has caused monarch populations to decline dramatically in recent decades. Conservation efforts are now underway to protect their migration routes."},
"nature-5":{title:"How Do Volcanoes Form?",source:"Wikipedia: Volcano",text:"A volcano is an opening in the Earth's crust through which molten rock, ash, and gases escape. Volcanoes form where tectonic plates meet. Most volcanoes are found along the Pacific Ring of Fire, a horseshoe-shaped region around the Pacific Ocean. When two plates collide, one plate is pushed beneath the other into the Earth's hot interior. The rock melts and rises to the surface as magma. When it reaches the surface, it is called lava. Some volcanoes erupt explosively, sending ash and rock high into the atmosphere. Others produce gentle flows of lava that slowly build up the landscape. Volcanic eruptions can be destructive, but they also create new land. The Hawaiian Islands were formed entirely by volcanic activity. Volcanic ash enriches soil, making the land around volcanoes some of the most fertile farmland on Earth."},
"nature-6":{title:"The Deep Ocean",source:"NOAA ocean exploration",text:"More than 80 percent of the ocean remains unexplored. The deep sea is the largest habitat on Earth, yet we know less about it than we do about the surface of Mars. In the deepest parts of the ocean, the pressure is over a thousand times greater than at the surface. The temperature is near freezing. Sunlight never reaches these depths. Yet life thrives here. Deep-sea creatures have evolved remarkable adaptations. Some produce their own light through bioluminescence. Others have enormous mouths and stretchable stomachs to make the most of rare meals. Hydrothermal vents on the ocean floor spew superheated, mineral-rich water. Entire ecosystems have developed around these vents, powered not by sunlight but by chemicals from the Earth's interior. New deep-sea species are discovered on almost every expedition."},
"nature-7":{title:"How Plants Communicate",source:"Scientific research, plant biology",text:"Plants may seem silent and passive, but research shows they communicate with each other in sophisticated ways. When a plant is attacked by insects, it releases chemical signals into the air. Nearby plants detect these signals and start producing defensive chemicals to protect themselves. Plants also communicate through their roots. They are connected by underground networks of fungi that scientists call the wood wide web. Through these networks, trees can share water and nutrients. A mother tree can send carbon to its seedlings through fungal connections. Plants can even warn each other of threats through these root networks. Some scientists believe that plants have a kind of intelligence, though very different from animal intelligence. They can learn from experience, remember past events, and make decisions based on information from their environment."},
"nature-8":{title:"The Northern Lights",source:"Wikipedia: Aurora",text:"The Northern Lights, or aurora borealis, are one of nature's most spectacular displays. They appear as curtains of green, pink, and purple light dancing across the night sky near the Earth's poles. The lights are caused by charged particles from the sun. When these particles reach Earth, they are guided by the planet's magnetic field toward the poles. There, they collide with atoms in the upper atmosphere. These collisions release energy in the form of light. Different gases produce different colors. Oxygen produces green and red light. Nitrogen produces blue and purple. The best places to see the Northern Lights are in northern Norway, Sweden, Finland, Canada, and Alaska. The lights are most active during solar storms, when the sun releases larger amounts of charged particles."},
"nature-9":{title:"The Incredible Octopus",source:"Wikipedia: Octopus",text:"Octopuses are among the most intelligent creatures in the ocean. They have large brains, excellent eyesight, and the ability to solve complex problems. In laboratory experiments, octopuses have opened jars, navigated mazes, and even recognized individual humans. An octopus has three hearts and blue blood. Its blood uses copper to carry oxygen, which is more efficient than iron in cold water. Octopuses are masters of camouflage. They can change the color, pattern, and even texture of their skin in less than a second to match their surroundings. If a predator gets too close, an octopus can release a cloud of ink to create a distraction and jet away. Most octopus species live only one to two years. The female lays thousands of eggs and guards them without eating until they hatch. She dies shortly after her young emerge."},
"nature-10":{title:"Why Leaves Change Color in Autumn",source:"Wikipedia: Autumn leaf color",text:"Every autumn, deciduous trees put on a spectacular show of red, orange, and yellow. This color change is not random. It is part of a carefully timed process that prepares trees for winter. During spring and summer, leaves are green because they contain high levels of chlorophyll, the pigment that captures sunlight for photosynthesis. As days grow shorter and temperatures drop, trees stop producing chlorophyll. The existing chlorophyll breaks down, revealing other pigments that were there all along. Carotenoids produce yellow and orange colors. Anthocyanins, which are produced in autumn, create red and purple hues. The intensity of autumn colors depends on weather. Bright, sunny days and cool nights produce the most vibrant displays. Once the leaves have transferred their remaining nutrients back to the tree, they fall to the ground."},
"nature-11":{title:"The World's Largest Rainforest",source:"Wikipedia: Amazon rainforest",text:"The Amazon rainforest is the largest tropical rainforest on Earth. It covers about 5.5 million square kilometers across nine South American countries. The Amazon is home to an incredible diversity of life. One in ten known species on Earth lives in the Amazon. A single hectare can contain over 750 types of trees. The Amazon plays a crucial role in regulating the global climate. Its trees absorb billions of tons of carbon dioxide. It produces about 20 percent of the world's oxygen. For this reason, it is sometimes called the lungs of the Earth. However, the Amazon is under threat. Deforestation for cattle ranching and soybean farming has destroyed large areas of the forest. Scientists warn that if deforestation continues, the Amazon could reach a tipping point where it can no longer sustain itself as a rainforest."},
"nature-12":{title:"How Birds Navigate During Migration",source:"Scientific research, ornithology",text:"Every year, billions of birds migrate between their breeding and wintering grounds. Some travel mind-boggling distances. The Arctic tern flies from the Arctic to the Antarctic and back each year, a round trip of about 70,000 kilometers. Birds use multiple navigation methods. They can sense the Earth's magnetic field through special cells in their eyes and beaks. This gives them an internal compass. They also use the sun during the day and the stars at night. Some birds can even see patterns of polarized light in the sky that humans cannot perceive. Memory plays a role too. Older birds that have made the journey before are better at navigating than young birds making their first migration. They remember landmarks like coastlines and mountain ranges. Scientists are still discovering new aspects of bird navigation, including evidence that some birds may use smell to find their way."},
"nature-13":{title:"What Is Lightning?",source:"Wikipedia: Lightning",text:"Lightning is a massive electrical discharge that occurs during thunderstorms. A single lightning bolt can contain up to one billion volts of electricity and heat the surrounding air to 30,000 degrees Celsius, which is five times hotter than the surface of the sun. Lightning forms when ice particles collide inside a storm cloud. These collisions create an electrical charge. Positive charges gather at the top of the cloud and negative charges at the bottom. When the difference becomes large enough, a lightning bolt jumps between the cloud and the ground, or between two clouds. The thunder we hear after lightning is caused by the rapid expansion of superheated air. Light travels faster than sound, which is why we see the lightning before we hear the thunder. You can estimate how far away a storm is by counting the seconds between the flash and the thunder. Every five seconds equals about one mile of distance."},
"nature-14":{title:"The Secret World of Fungi",source:"Scientific research, mycology",text:"Fungi are neither plants nor animals. They belong to their own kingdom of life. The mushrooms we see above ground are just the fruit of a much larger organism. Underground, fungi form vast networks of thread-like structures called mycelium. A single fungus in Oregon covers almost ten square kilometers and may be thousands of years old, making it one of the largest and oldest living organisms on Earth. Fungi play essential roles in ecosystems. They decompose dead matter and recycle nutrients. They form partnerships with the roots of most plants, helping them absorb water and minerals. Without fungi, forests as we know them could not exist. Fungi have also given us some of our most important medicines. Penicillin, the first antibiotic, was discovered from a mold fungus. Fungi are used to make bread, beer, cheese, and many other foods."},
"nature-15":{title:"How Glaciers Shape the Landscape",source:"Wikipedia: Glacier",text:"Glaciers are massive rivers of ice that flow slowly across the land. They form in places where more snow falls in winter than melts in summer. Over many years, the accumulated snow compresses into solid ice. Glaciers cover about 10 percent of the Earth's land surface and hold about 69 percent of the world's fresh water. As glaciers move, they reshape the landscape. They carve out valleys, grind down mountains, and transport enormous boulders over long distances. Many of the world's most dramatic landscapes were sculpted by glaciers during the last ice age. Today, most glaciers are shrinking due to climate change. This melting contributes to rising sea levels. The loss of glaciers also threatens water supplies for millions of people who depend on seasonal meltwater for drinking and farming."},

// ===== 科学科技 16-25 =====
"science-16":{title:"How Do Touchscreens Work?",source:"Wikipedia: Touchscreen",text:"Every time you tap your phone screen, you are completing an electrical circuit. Most modern smartphones use capacitive touchscreens. The screen is coated with a transparent conductor. Your finger carries a tiny electrical charge. When you touch the screen, it draws a small current from each corner. The processor measures the current from each corner and uses the differences to calculate the exact position of your touch. This all happens in milliseconds. Some touchscreens can detect multiple fingers at once, which enables pinch-to-zoom gestures. Older resistive touchscreens used pressure instead of electricity. They were less responsive and could not handle multi-touch. The first capacitive touchscreen was invented in 1965, decades before smartphones made them ubiquitous."},
"science-17":{title:"What Is Quantum Computing?",source:"Wikipedia: Quantum computing",text:"A quantum computer uses the strange properties of quantum mechanics to solve problems that classical computers cannot. Regular computers use bits that are either 0 or 1. Quantum computers use quantum bits, or qubits, which can be both 0 and 1 at the same time. This property is called superposition. Another property, called entanglement, allows qubits to be linked so that the state of one instantly affects another, no matter how far apart they are. These properties let quantum computers explore many possible solutions simultaneously. In 2019, Google claimed its quantum processor solved a problem in 200 seconds that would take the fastest classical supercomputer 10,000 years. Quantum computers could revolutionize drug discovery, materials science, and cryptography. However, the technology is still in its early stages and faces enormous engineering challenges."},
"science-18":{title:"The Science of Rainbows",source:"Wikipedia: Rainbow",text:"A rainbow forms when sunlight passes through raindrops. As light enters a drop, it slows down and bends. This is called refraction. Different colors bend by different amounts. Violet light bends the most and red light bends the least. The light then reflects off the back of the drop and refracts again as it exits. This double refraction separates white light into its component colors. A primary rainbow always appears at an angle of about 42 degrees from the direction opposite the sun. The colors always appear in the same order: red on the outside, followed by orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo, and violet. Sometimes you can see a secondary rainbow outside the primary one. It is fainter and has reversed colors. The dark band between them is called Alexander's band."},
"science-19":{title:"How Your Voice Reaches Across the World",source:"Wikipedia: Mobile telephony",text:"When you speak into your phone, your voice is converted into digital data. A microphone turns sound waves into electrical signals. A chip then converts these analog signals into a stream of zeros and ones. This digital data is compressed and broken into small packets. Each packet carries a piece of your voice along with information about where it came from and where it is going. The packets travel through fiber optic cables, cell towers, and sometimes even satellites. They may take different routes across the network. At the receiving end, the packets are reassembled in the correct order. A chip converts the digital data back into electrical signals, and a speaker turns those signals back into sound waves. The entire process takes less than a tenth of a second, which is why phone conversations feel instantaneous."},
"science-20":{title:"The Search for Exoplanets",source:"Wikipedia: Exoplanet",text:"An exoplanet is a planet that orbits a star other than our sun. Before 1992, no one knew if such planets existed. Today, astronomers have confirmed over 5,000 exoplanets. Most are detected indirectly. The transit method watches for tiny dips in a star's brightness when a planet passes in front of it. The radial velocity method detects the slight wobble of a star caused by an orbiting planet's gravity. Some exoplanets are rocky like Earth. Others are gas giants many times the size of Jupiter. A few orbit in the habitable zone, the region where temperatures could allow liquid water to exist. The James Webb Space Telescope, launched in 2021, can analyze the atmospheres of distant exoplanets. Finding signs of oxygen, methane, or water vapor would be a major step toward answering the question of whether we are alone in the universe."},
"science-21":{title:"What Makes Popcorn Pop?",source:"Scientific American",text:"Each kernel of popcorn contains a small amount of water trapped inside a hard shell. When you heat the kernel, the water turns into steam. The pressure inside builds to about nine times normal atmospheric pressure. At around 180 degrees Celsius, the shell suddenly ruptures. The starch inside expands explosively, turning inside out and cooling into the fluffy shape we know. The whole process takes a fraction of a second. Scientists have used high-speed cameras to film popcorn popping at thousands of frames per second. They discovered that the kernel actually does a little jump as it pops. The ideal popping temperature and moisture content have been carefully studied. Too dry and the kernel will not pop at all. Too moist and it will pop into a dense, chewy ball rather than the light and crispy snack we enjoy."},
"science-22":{title:"The Mystery of Antimatter",source:"Wikipedia: Antimatter",text:"Every particle of matter has a corresponding antimatter particle. The electron has the positron. The proton has the antiproton. When matter and antimatter meet, they annihilate each other, releasing pure energy. According to the laws of physics, the Big Bang should have created equal amounts of matter and antimatter. Yet the universe we see is made almost entirely of matter. Where did all the antimatter go? This is one of the biggest unsolved mysteries in physics. Scientists at CERN have been studying tiny differences in the behavior of matter and antimatter particles. These differences might explain why matter came to dominate. Antimatter also has practical applications. PET scans, a common medical imaging technique, use positrons to detect diseases inside the body."},
"science-23":{title:"How Does a Microwave Heat Food?",source:"Wikipedia: Microwave oven",text:"A microwave oven heats food using electromagnetic waves. These waves have a frequency of about 2.45 gigahertz. At this frequency, they cause water molecules to rotate rapidly back and forth. The friction between these vibrating molecules generates heat. This is why foods with high water content heat up quickly in a microwave. Metal reflects microwaves, which is why you should not put metal objects inside a microwave. The waves bounce off the metal walls of the oven and penetrate the food from multiple directions. This is also why the oven has a turntable. Without rotation, some spots would receive more energy than others, creating hot and cold patches. The microwave was invented accidentally in 1945 when an engineer noticed that a chocolate bar in his pocket had melted while he was working with radar equipment."},
"science-24":{title:"The Future of Space Travel",source:"NASA and SpaceX",text:"Space travel is entering a new era. For decades, only governments could afford to send humans into space. Today, private companies like SpaceX and Blue Origin are building reusable rockets that dramatically lower the cost of reaching orbit. SpaceX's Starship, the largest rocket ever built, is designed to carry up to 100 people to Mars. NASA's Artemis program plans to return humans to the Moon and establish a permanent lunar base. The challenges of deep space travel remain formidable. Astronauts face radiation exposure, muscle and bone loss, and psychological stress from long periods of isolation. Engineers are developing new propulsion technologies, including ion drives and nuclear thermal rockets, that could shorten travel times. The dream of becoming a multiplanetary species is closer to reality than at any point in human history."},
"science-25":{title:"What Causes the Seasons?",source:"Wikipedia: Season",text:"Many people believe that summer is warmer because the Earth is closer to the sun. This is incorrect. The seasons are caused by the tilt of the Earth's axis. Our planet spins at an angle of about 23.5 degrees relative to its orbit around the sun. When the Northern Hemisphere is tilted toward the sun, it receives more direct sunlight and experiences summer. At the same time, the Southern Hemisphere is tilted away and experiences winter. Six months later, the situation reverses. This is why seasons are opposite in the two hemispheres. The distance between the Earth and the sun actually changes by about 5 million kilometers over the course of a year, but this has very little effect on temperatures compared to the tilt."},

// ===== 健康 16-25 =====
"health-16":{title:"The Importance of Vitamin D",source:"NIH research",text:"Vitamin D is often called the sunshine vitamin because your skin produces it when exposed to sunlight. It helps your body absorb calcium, which is essential for strong bones. Without enough vitamin D, bones can become thin and brittle. Vitamin D also supports the immune system and may help protect against certain diseases. Many people do not get enough vitamin D, especially in winter or in northern countries where sunlight is weak. Fatty fish like salmon, egg yolks, and fortified milk are good dietary sources. However, it is difficult to get enough vitamin D from food alone. Spending 15 to 20 minutes in the sun a few times a week is usually enough for most people. Vitamin D deficiency can cause fatigue, bone pain, and muscle weakness. A simple blood test can check your levels."},
"health-17":{title:"How Antibiotics Work",source:"Wikipedia: Antibiotic",text:"Antibiotics are medicines that kill bacteria or stop them from multiplying. The first antibiotic, penicillin, was discovered by Alexander Fleming in 1928 when he noticed that a mold had killed bacteria in one of his laboratory dishes. Antibiotics work in several ways. Some attack the cell wall of bacteria, causing them to burst. Others interfere with the bacteria's ability to make proteins or copy their DNA. Antibiotics only work against bacteria, not viruses. This is why they are useless for colds and flu. Overuse of antibiotics has led to the rise of antibiotic-resistant bacteria, sometimes called superbugs. These resistant strains can survive treatments that would have killed them in the past. To slow the development of resistance, doctors now prescribe antibiotics more cautiously and patients are told to complete the full course even if they feel better."},
"health-18":{title:"What Happens When You Have a Fever",source:"Medical research",text:"A fever is not an illness in itself. It is a defense mechanism. When your body detects an infection, your immune system releases chemicals that signal your brain to raise your body temperature. This higher temperature makes it harder for bacteria and viruses to reproduce. It also speeds up your immune response. Most fevers are harmless and do not need treatment. However, very high fevers above 40 degrees Celsius can be dangerous, especially for young children. The old advice to starve a fever has no scientific basis. Your body needs energy to fight infection, so eating healthy food and drinking plenty of fluids is important. Fever-reducing medicines like paracetamol can help with discomfort but may slightly prolong the illness by interfering with the body's natural defense."},
"health-19":{title:"The Gut-Brain Connection",source:"Harvard Medical School",text:"Your gut and your brain are connected by a large nerve called the vagus nerve. This is the main pathway of the gut-brain axis. The trillions of bacteria living in your intestines produce neurotransmitters, including serotonin, dopamine, and GABA. These chemicals influence your mood, anxiety levels, and cognitive function. About 90 percent of serotonin is produced in the gut, not the brain. An unbalanced gut microbiome has been linked to depression, anxiety, and even autism spectrum disorders. On the other hand, a healthy diet rich in fiber and fermented foods promotes beneficial bacteria. Probiotics are live bacteria found in yogurt, kimchi, and other fermented foods. Scientists are now studying whether treating the gut microbiome could become a new approach to mental health care."},
"health-20":{title:"Why Handwashing Works",source:"CDC and WHO guidelines",text:"Washing your hands with soap is one of the most effective ways to prevent the spread of disease. Soap molecules have a unique structure. One end is attracted to water and the other end is attracted to fats and oils. Many viruses and bacteria are surrounded by a fatty membrane. The soap molecules pull this membrane apart, destroying the pathogen. The friction of rubbing your hands together helps dislodge germs from the skin. Rinsing with water washes everything away. Alcohol-based hand sanitizers work differently. They denature the proteins of germs, effectively killing them. However, sanitizers do not remove dirt and are less effective on visibly soiled hands. The World Health Organization recommends washing hands for at least 20 seconds, which is roughly the time it takes to sing Happy Birthday twice."},
"health-21":{title:"The Science of Stretching",source:"Sports medicine research",text:"Stretching feels good, but what is actually happening inside your muscles? When you stretch, you are lengthening the muscle fibers and the connective tissue that surrounds them. Your muscles contain sensors called spindles that detect changes in length. When a muscle stretches too fast, the spindle triggers a reflex that makes the muscle contract to protect itself. This is why slow, steady stretching is more effective than bouncing. Regular stretching increases flexibility by training the nervous system to tolerate greater muscle extension. It also improves blood flow to the muscles. Research shows that static stretching before exercise may slightly reduce strength and power. For this reason, many athletes now do dynamic stretches as part of their warm-up and save static stretching for after the workout."},
"health-22":{title:"How the Body Heals a Cut",source:"Medical research, wound healing",text:"When you get a cut, your body immediately begins a complex repair process. First, blood platelets rush to the wound and form a clot to stop the bleeding. Next, the immune system sends white blood cells to clean the area and fight any bacteria that entered. This is why cuts often become red and slightly swollen. Then, specialized cells called fibroblasts produce collagen, a protein that forms the scaffolding for new tissue. New blood vessels grow into the area to supply oxygen and nutrients. Finally, the outer layer of skin regenerates to close the wound. The whole process can take days to weeks depending on the size and depth of the cut. Keeping a wound clean and moist actually speeds up healing, contrary to the old belief that wounds should be left to dry out."},
"health-23":{title:"The Power of Music Therapy",source:"American Music Therapy Association",text:"Music therapy uses music to address physical, emotional, and cognitive needs. It is practiced by trained therapists who use listening, singing, and playing instruments as therapeutic tools. Research has shown that music therapy can reduce pain and anxiety in hospital patients. It helps stroke survivors regain speech by engaging brain regions that are undamaged. For people with dementia, familiar songs can unlock memories and emotions that seem otherwise lost. Music stimulates the release of dopamine, the same chemical involved in pleasure and reward. Playing music with others has been found to synchronize brainwaves and create a sense of social bonding. Unlike passive listening, active music therapy involves making music, which engages more areas of the brain and produces stronger therapeutic effects."},
"health-24":{title:"What Are Calories?",source:"Nutrition science",text:"A calorie is a unit of energy. Specifically, it is the amount of energy needed to raise the temperature of one gram of water by one degree Celsius. When we talk about food, we actually mean kilocalories, each equal to 1,000 calories. Your body uses calories for everything: breathing, pumping blood, thinking, and moving. The number of calories you need depends on your age, gender, weight, and activity level. An average adult needs about 2,000 to 2,500 calories per day. If you consume more calories than you burn, the excess is stored as fat. If you consume fewer, your body burns stored fat for energy. Not all calories are equal. Calories from protein keep you full longer than the same number of calories from sugar. This is why diet quality matters as much as calorie counting."},
"health-25":{title:"The Importance of Posture",source:"Orthopedic research",text:"Good posture is about more than looking confident. It affects your breathing, digestion, and even your mood. When you slouch, your rib cage compresses your lungs, reducing your oxygen intake by up to 30 percent. This can lead to fatigue and difficulty concentrating. Poor posture also puts extra strain on your spine. For every inch your head tilts forward, the effective weight on your neck increases by about 10 pounds. Over time, this can lead to chronic neck and back pain. The rise of smartphones has created a new problem called text neck. The good news is that posture can be improved with awareness and exercise. Strengthening your core muscles helps support your spine. Regular breaks from sitting and adjusting your screen height can also make a big difference."},

// ===== 生活 16-25 =====
"life-16":{title:"The Five-Minute Rule for Motivation",source:"Productivity research",text:"Starting is always the hardest part. The five-minute rule is simple: commit to doing something for just five minutes. After five minutes, you are allowed to stop. More often than not, once you have started, you will want to continue. This works because most resistance is emotional, not physical. Your brain exaggerates how unpleasant a task will be. Taking the first small step breaks the illusion. Writers use this trick by committing to write just one sentence. Runners use it by putting on their shoes without worrying about the run itself. The rule works with any task you have been avoiding. The key is that the commitment must be genuinely small and achievable. Telling yourself you will study for five minutes is believable. Telling yourself you will study for three hours is not."},
"life-17":{title:"The Art of Listening",source:"Communication studies",text:"Most people do not actually listen. They wait for their turn to speak. Real listening is an active skill that takes practice. It means giving your full attention to the other person without planning your response. Active listening involves three things: paying attention, showing that you are listening, and responding thoughtfully. You can show you are listening by nodding, maintaining eye contact, and occasionally summarizing what the other person said. Good listeners ask follow-up questions rather than immediately sharing their own experience. This makes the speaker feel heard and valued. Research shows that people who feel listened to have lower stress levels and stronger relationships. In a world where everyone wants to be heard, being the person who truly listens is a rare and valuable skill."},
"life-18":{title:"How to Apologize Sincerely",source:"Psychology research",text:"A bad apology can make things worse. I am sorry you feel that way is not an apology. It shifts blame to the other person's feelings. A sincere apology has four parts: acknowledge what you did wrong, express genuine regret, offer to make things right, and explain how you will prevent it from happening again. Do not include excuses or justifications. When you say I am sorry, but you were also wrong, you cancel the apology. The most important part is taking full responsibility for your actions. A sincere apology restores trust and can actually strengthen a relationship. Research shows that people who apologize are seen as more competent and trustworthy, not less. Admitting you were wrong takes courage, and people respect that."},
"life-19":{title:"Why Multitasking Does Not Work",source:"Cognitive neuroscience",text:"Many people pride themselves on their ability to multitask. However, research shows that the brain cannot actually focus on two cognitive tasks at once. What we call multitasking is really task switching. The brain rapidly shifts attention back and forth between tasks. Each switch comes with a cost. It takes time to refocus, and errors increase. Studies have found that heavy multitaskers perform worse on tests of attention and memory than people who focus on one thing at a time. The brain's prefrontal cortex can only handle one complex task at a time. Activities like walking and talking are different because they use separate brain systems. For tasks that require thinking, single-tasking is far more efficient. Turn off notifications, close extra tabs, and give one task your full attention. You will finish faster and make fewer mistakes."},
"life-20":{title:"How to Read Faster Without Losing Comprehension",source:"Cognitive psychology",text:"Speed reading courses promise to double or triple your reading speed. But can you really read thousands of words per minute and still understand them? The science says no. Reading speed is fundamentally limited by the biology of the eye and the brain. The eye can only focus on a small area at a time. Skimming and skipping words inevitably reduces comprehension. However, there are ways to read more efficiently without sacrificing understanding. One technique is to stop subvocalizing, or silently pronouncing each word in your head. While complete elimination is impossible, reducing subvocalization can increase speed. Another technique is to preview the material first. Read the title, headings, and first sentences of paragraphs before diving in. This gives your brain a framework for organizing the information that follows."},
"life-21":{title:"The Power of a Growth Mindset",source:"Carol Dweck, Stanford University",text:"Psychologist Carol Dweck identified two types of mindsets: fixed and growth. People with a fixed mindset believe that intelligence and talent are static. They avoid challenges because failure would prove they are not smart. People with a growth mindset believe that abilities can be developed through effort and learning. They embrace challenges as opportunities to grow. These two mindsets lead to very different outcomes. Students with a growth mindset earn higher grades and bounce back from setbacks faster. The good news is that mindsets themselves can be changed. Simply learning about the growth mindset and understanding that the brain can build new connections throughout life can shift how you approach challenges. Praising effort rather than intelligence encourages a growth mindset in children and adults alike."},
"life-22":{title:"How to Network Without Feeling Fake",source:"Career development research",text:"Networking makes many people uncomfortable. It can feel insincere and transactional. But genuine networking is not about collecting contacts or asking for favors. It is about building real relationships based on mutual interest and help. The best networkers give more than they take. They share knowledge, make introductions, and offer help without expecting immediate returns. Start by reconnecting with people you already know. Ask about their work and listen. When you meet someone new, be curious about what they do and why they find it interesting. Do not try to impress. Just be genuinely interested. The most valuable networking conversations often happen naturally, not at formal events. A quick coffee with a classmate or a thoughtful comment on someone's work can be the start of a meaningful connection."},
"life-23":{title:"The Benefits of Learning an Instrument",source:"Neuroscience research",text:"Learning to play a musical instrument is one of the best things you can do for your brain. Playing music engages nearly every region of the brain simultaneously. It strengthens connections between the left and right hemispheres. Musicians have been found to have larger and more connected brains than non-musicians. The benefits go beyond music. Children who learn an instrument tend to perform better in math and reading. Adults who play music have slower age-related cognitive decline. Learning an instrument teaches discipline, patience, and the value of consistent practice. It also provides a healthy outlet for emotional expression. You do not need to be a prodigy. Even beginners show measurable brain changes after a few months of regular practice. The key is consistency, not talent."},
"life-24":{title:"How to Argue Better",source:"Conflict resolution research",text:"Most arguments are not about finding truth. They are about winning. But winning an argument often means losing a relationship. Constructive disagreement is a skill. Start by listening carefully to the other person's position. Repeat it back in your own words before responding. This shows respect and ensures you actually understand. Use I statements instead of you accusations. I feel frustrated when is better than You always make me angry. Avoid words like always and never, which are rarely accurate and put the other person on the defensive. Focus on the specific issue, not the person's character. Know when to take a break. When emotions run high, the rational part of your brain shuts down. A 20-minute pause can reset the conversation. The goal is not to defeat the other person but to reach understanding."},
"life-25":{title:"The Value of Volunteering",source:"Social psychology research",text:"Volunteering is not just good for the community. It is good for you. Studies show that people who volunteer regularly have lower rates of depression, better physical health, and greater life satisfaction. Helping others activates the brain's reward system. It creates a sense of purpose that money and career success cannot provide. Volunteering also builds skills and connections. You learn to work with diverse groups of people and solve real-world problems. These experiences are valuable in any career. The type of volunteering matters less than the consistency. Whether you tutor students, clean up parks, or visit elderly neighbors, doing it regularly is more important than doing it intensively. Even a few hours a month can make a measurable difference in your well-being and in the lives of others."},

// ===== 文化 16-25 =====
"culture-16":{title:"The Dead Sea Scrolls",source:"Wikipedia: Dead Sea Scrolls",text:"In 1947, a young shepherd searching for a lost goat stumbled upon one of the greatest archaeological discoveries of the 20th century. Inside a cave near the Dead Sea, he found clay jars containing ancient scrolls. Over the following decade, more caves were searched and thousands of scroll fragments were recovered. The scrolls include the oldest known copies of the Hebrew Bible, dating back over 2,000 years. They also contain previously unknown religious texts, community rules, and hymns. The scrolls were likely written by a Jewish sect called the Essenes. They hid the documents in caves to protect them during a time of conflict. The scrolls have transformed our understanding of the development of the Bible and the diversity of Jewish thought during the time of Jesus."},
"culture-17":{title:"The Origins of Halloween",source:"Wikipedia: Halloween",text:"Halloween as we know it today is a blend of ancient Celtic and Christian traditions. It originated with the Celtic festival of Samhain, celebrated on October 31st. The Celts believed that on this night, the boundary between the world of the living and the dead became blurred. People lit bonfires and wore costumes to ward off ghosts. When Christianity spread to Celtic lands, the church designated November 1st as All Saints Day. The night before became known as All Hallows Eve, which eventually became Halloween. Irish immigrants brought Halloween traditions to America in the 19th century. Trick-or-treating evolved from the medieval practice of souling, where poor people would go door to door offering prayers for the dead in exchange for food. Today, Halloween is a multi-billion dollar industry celebrated in many countries around the world."},
"culture-18":{title:"The Great Wall of China",source:"Wikipedia: Great Wall of China",text:"The Great Wall is not a single wall but a series of fortifications built over nearly 2,000 years by different Chinese dynasties. The earliest sections were built in the 7th century BCE. The most famous sections were constructed during the Ming Dynasty between the 14th and 17th centuries. The total length of all sections is over 21,000 kilometers. Contrary to popular belief, the Great Wall cannot be seen from space with the naked eye. It is simply too narrow. The wall was built to protect against invasions from the north. It was made from whatever materials were available locally: stone, brick, wood, and even packed earth. Millions of workers, including soldiers, peasants, and prisoners, labored on the wall over the centuries. Many died and were buried within it. Today, the Great Wall is a UNESCO World Heritage site and a symbol of Chinese civilization."},
"culture-19":{title:"Why We Love Stories About Heroes",source:"Comparative mythology",text:"Every culture on Earth tells stories about heroes. From ancient myths to modern superhero movies, the pattern is remarkably similar. The scholar Joseph Campbell called this pattern the hero's journey. A hero leaves the ordinary world, faces challenges, receives help from mentors, confronts a great evil, and returns transformed. This pattern appears in the stories of Buddha, Jesus, Harry Potter, and Luke Skywalker. Why is this pattern so universal? Some psychologists believe it reflects the basic arc of human development. We all leave the safety of childhood, face challenges in the adult world, and hopefully return with wisdom to share. Stories of heroes inspire us because they show us that transformation is possible. They remind us that ordinary people can rise to meet extraordinary challenges."},
"culture-20":{title:"The History of Zero",source:"Wikipedia: History of zero",text:"Zero seems obvious to us today. But the concept of zero as a number was a revolutionary idea. Ancient civilizations had no symbol for nothing. The Babylonians used a placeholder symbol but did not treat it as a number. The concept of zero as a full number was developed in India around the 5th century CE. The Indian mathematician Brahmagupta wrote rules for arithmetic with zero, including the puzzling fact that zero divided by any number is zero. From India, the concept spread to the Islamic world and then to Europe. The Arabic word for zero is sifr, which became cipher and zero in European languages. The invention of zero made modern mathematics possible. It enabled the development of algebra, calculus, and eventually the binary code that powers all digital computers."},
"culture-21":{title:"The Silk Road",source:"Wikipedia: Silk Road",text:"The Silk Road was not a single road but a network of trade routes connecting China to the Mediterranean. It operated for over 1,500 years. Silk was just one of many goods traded. Spices, precious stones, glassware, and ideas all traveled along these routes. Religions spread along the Silk Road too. Buddhism traveled from India to China. Islam spread into Central Asia. The route was dangerous. Merchants faced bandits, extreme weather, and vast deserts. Few traveled the entire length. Instead, goods passed through many hands, increasing in price at each stop. The Silk Road declined after the 15th century as sea routes became cheaper and safer. Today, China's Belt and Road Initiative is sometimes called the new Silk Road, a modern echo of the ancient trade network that connected civilizations."},
"culture-22":{title:"How Chess Conquered the World",source:"Wikipedia: History of chess",text:"Chess originated in India around the 6th century CE. It was called chaturanga and represented a battle between four divisions of an army: infantry, cavalry, elephants, and chariots. The game spread to Persia, where it became known as shatranj. The Arabs brought chess to Europe through Spain. Over centuries, the rules evolved. The queen and bishop became more powerful in the 15th century, transforming the game from a slow strategic exercise into a faster and more dynamic contest. The first World Chess Championship was held in 1886. In 1997, a computer named Deep Blue defeated the reigning world champion Garry Kasparov. Today, chess is played by hundreds of millions of people. The internet has made it possible to play against anyone in the world at any time."},
"culture-23":{title:"The Mystery of Stonehenge",source:"Wikipedia: Stonehenge",text:"Stonehenge is a prehistoric monument on Salisbury Plain in England. It consists of massive stones arranged in concentric circles. The largest stones weigh over 25 tons and were transported from up to 250 kilometers away. How did people move such enormous stones without modern machinery? Several theories exist, including sledges, rollers, and even water transport. But how is less mysterious than why. Archaeologists believe Stonehenge was a ceremonial site aligned with the movements of the sun. On the summer solstice, the sun rises directly over the Heel Stone and shines into the center of the monument. Some believe it was also a burial site, as cremated human remains have been found there. Stonehenge was built in stages over about 1,500 years, starting around 3,000 BCE. It remains one of the world's most enigmatic ancient structures."},
"culture-24":{title:"The Eiffel Tower",source:"Wikipedia: Eiffel Tower",text:"The Eiffel Tower was built for the 1889 World's Fair in Paris. It was designed by the engineer Gustave Eiffel. At the time, many Parisians hated it. A group of prominent artists and writers called it a monstrous eyesore. The tower was meant to be temporary and was nearly torn down in 1909. It was saved because it proved useful as a radio transmission tower. During World War I, its radio equipment intercepted enemy communications. Today, the Eiffel Tower is the most visited paid monument in the world. It welcomes nearly seven million visitors each year. The tower is 330 meters tall and was the tallest structure in the world until 1930. It is repainted every seven years, a job that requires 60 tons of paint. The tower's height changes by about 15 centimeters between summer and winter due to the thermal expansion of the iron."},
"culture-25":{title:"The Invention of Paper",source:"Wikipedia: History of paper",text:"Paper was invented in China around 105 CE by a court official named Cai Lun. Before paper, people wrote on bones, bamboo strips, silk, and papyrus. Cai Lun's method used tree bark, hemp, rags, and fishing nets. The materials were soaked, beaten into a pulp, and pressed into thin sheets. The invention was so important that Cai Lun was later deified as the god of papermaking. Paper gradually replaced bamboo and silk for writing in China. The technology spread slowly. It reached Korea and Japan in the 7th century and the Islamic world in the 8th century. Europeans learned papermaking from the Arabs and built the first paper mills in the 12th century. The invention of the printing press in the 15th century dramatically increased demand for paper. Today, the global paper industry produces over 400 million tons annually."},

// ===== 自然 16-25 =====
"nature-16":{title:"The Life of a Honeybee",source:"Wikipedia: Honey bee life cycle",text:"A worker honeybee lives only about six weeks during the busy summer season. In that short life, she takes on a series of different jobs. For the first three days, she cleans cells in the hive. Then she becomes a nurse bee, feeding larvae with a substance she produces from glands in her head. After about a week, she begins producing wax and building honeycomb. Next, she guards the hive entrance, fighting off intruders. Finally, at about three weeks old, she becomes a forager, flying up to five kilometers to collect nectar and pollen. She will make about ten foraging trips per day. A single bee produces only about one-twelfth of a teaspoon of honey in her entire life. Yet a healthy hive can produce over 25 kilograms of honey in a season through the combined efforts of tens of thousands of workers."},
"nature-17":{title:"The World's Fastest Animals",source:"Wikipedia: Fastest animals",text:"The cheetah is famous as the fastest land animal, reaching speeds of up to 120 kilometers per hour in short bursts. But it is not the fastest animal on Earth. The peregrine falcon dives at over 320 kilometers per hour, making it the fastest member of the animal kingdom. In the water, the sailfish can swim at 110 kilometers per hour. Among insects, the dragonfly holds the record at about 55 kilometers per hour. Speed in nature is not just about raw power. The cheetah's flexible spine, large nostrils, and non-retractable claws are all adaptations for sprinting. The falcon's streamlined body and special nose structures allow it to breathe during high-speed dives. Evolution has shaped each of these animals into a perfect machine for its particular hunting strategy."},
"nature-18":{title:"Why the Ocean Is Salty",source:"NOAA",text:"The salt in the ocean comes primarily from rocks on land. Rain is slightly acidic because it absorbs carbon dioxide from the air. As rain falls on rocks, it slowly dissolves tiny amounts of minerals. These dissolved minerals, including sodium and chloride, are carried by rivers to the ocean. Sodium and chloride make up about 85 percent of the salt in seawater. The ocean also receives minerals from hydrothermal vents on the seafloor. Over millions of years, water evaporates from the ocean surface, but the salt stays behind. This is why the ocean has become saltier over geological time. If all the salt in the ocean were spread over the land, it would form a layer about 150 meters thick. Some lakes, like the Dead Sea, are far saltier than the ocean because they have no outlet and water only escapes through evaporation."},
"nature-19":{title:"How Seeds Travel",source:"Botany research",text:"Plants cannot walk, but their seeds can travel remarkable distances. Different plants have evolved different strategies for dispersing their seeds. Dandelion seeds have tiny parachutes that catch the wind and can travel for kilometers. Coconut seeds are encased in tough, buoyant shells that can float across oceans to colonize new islands. Some plants use animals as transportation. Burdock seeds have hooks that cling to animal fur. Squirrels bury acorns and forget some of them, effectively planting oak trees. Some seeds even survive passing through an animal's digestive system and germinate in the droppings. The most extreme dispersal strategy belongs to the sandbox tree, whose seed pods explode with enough force to shoot seeds at 70 meters per second. This variety of strategies ensures that plants can spread their offspring to new locations where they might find better growing conditions."},
"nature-20":{title:"The Remarkable Communication of Dolphins",source:"Marine biology research",text:"Dolphins are among the most intelligent animals on Earth. They communicate using a complex system of clicks, whistles, and body language. Each dolphin has a unique signature whistle that functions like a name. Other dolphins can recognize and call out to specific individuals using these whistles. Dolphins also use echolocation to navigate and hunt. They produce clicking sounds that bounce off objects and return as echoes. From these echoes, a dolphin can determine the size, shape, distance, and even the internal structure of objects. Dolphins live in social groups called pods. They cooperate to hunt fish, protect each other from predators, and even babysit each other's calves. Scientists continue to study dolphin communication, and some believe that dolphins may have a form of language that we have yet to fully decode."},
"nature-21":{title:"The Secret of Gecko Feet",source:"Scientific research, biomimetics",text:"Geckos can walk up walls and across ceilings with ease. Their secret is in the structure of their feet. Each toe pad is covered with millions of tiny hair-like structures called setae. Each seta splits into hundreds of even smaller structures called spatulae. These structures are so small that they interact with surfaces at the molecular level through van der Waals forces. These are weak forces that arise from temporary fluctuations in electron clouds. Individually, each force is tiny. But multiplied across billions of contact points, they create a powerful adhesive effect. A gecko can support its entire body weight with a single toe. The feet are also self-cleaning. Dirt particles fall off as the gecko takes steps. Scientists are studying gecko feet to develop new types of adhesives, robots that can climb walls, and reusable tapes that leave no residue."},
"nature-22":{title:"How Animals Survive the Desert",source:"Desert ecology",text:"Deserts are among the harshest environments on Earth. Temperatures can exceed 50 degrees Celsius during the day and drop below freezing at night. Water is scarce. Yet many animals have evolved remarkable adaptations to survive here. The fennec fox has enormous ears that radiate heat and help it stay cool. The kangaroo rat never drinks water. It gets all the moisture it needs from the seeds it eats. Camels do not store water in their humps. The humps are fat reserves that can be metabolized into water and energy when food is scarce. Many desert animals are nocturnal, avoiding the extreme heat of the day. Some desert frogs spend most of the year buried underground in a cocoon of shed skin, emerging only when rare rains create temporary pools for breeding."},
"nature-23":{title:"Why Leaves Are Green",source:"Wikipedia: Chlorophyll",text:"Leaves are green because they contain a pigment called chlorophyll. Chlorophyll absorbs red and blue light from the sun but reflects green light, which is why plants appear green to our eyes. Chlorophyll is essential for photosynthesis. It captures light energy and uses it to convert carbon dioxide and water into glucose and oxygen. There are actually several types of chlorophyll. The most common types are chlorophyll a and chlorophyll b. Other pigments are present in leaves too. Carotenoids reflect yellow and orange light. In most of the year, chlorophyll dominates and hides these other colors. In autumn, as days shorten and temperatures drop, plants stop producing chlorophyll. The existing chlorophyll breaks down, and the underlying carotenoids become visible, creating the brilliant fall foliage. Scientists are still studying exactly why nature chose green as the dominant color for photosynthesis."},
"nature-24":{title:"The Journey of a Raindrop",source:"Hydrology",text:"The water in a single raindrop may have traveled an incredible journey. It might have evaporated from the Pacific Ocean, drifted across a continent as a cloud, and fallen on a forest thousands of kilometers away. From there, it could seep into the soil, be absorbed by tree roots, and be released back into the air through transpiration. Or it might flow into a stream and eventually return to the ocean. The average water molecule spends about nine days in the atmosphere before falling as rain. Once on the ground, it might spend weeks in a river, years in a lake, or millennia in a glacier or deep underground aquifer. The water you drink today contains molecules that dinosaurs drank millions of years ago. The Earth has a fixed amount of water. It constantly recycles through the water cycle, connecting every living thing on the planet."},
"nature-25":{title:"The Intelligence of Crows",source:"Animal cognition research",text:"Crows are among the smartest animals on the planet. They use tools, solve multi-step puzzles, and can even recognize individual human faces. In one experiment, crows bent a wire into a hook to retrieve food from a tube. This kind of tool creation was previously thought to be unique to humans and great apes. Crows can also plan for the future, saving tools they might need later. They remember people who have threatened them and can pass this knowledge to their offspring. Crows hold funerals for their dead, gathering around a deceased crow in what scientists believe is a form of social learning about danger. The brain of a crow is very different from a mammal brain, yet it achieves comparable intelligence through a completely different evolutionary path. This is an example of convergent evolution."}
};